WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.319
Back in the kitchen, our new cook has picked up a habit.

2
00:00:06.319 --> 00:00:12.145
When a recipe says, make the sauce on page 12, the cook doesn't flip to page 12 every time.

3
00:00:12.145 --> 00:00:15.154
The cook copies the sauce into the main recipe.

4
00:00:15.154 --> 00:00:17.971
Now the onions for both get chopped at once.

5
00:00:18.133 --> 00:00:22.739
Keep that cook in mind, because here's a question that sounds almost silly.

6
00:00:22.739 --> 00:00:25.072
Every call to a method costs a little.

7
00:00:25.072 --> 00:00:28.634
So how can splitting one method into two make code faster?

8
00:00:28.634 --> 00:00:29.616
Hold on to that.

9
00:00:31.333 --> 00:00:34.091
Let's start with something we've all written.

10
00:00:34.091 --> 00:00:37.094
The report service checks every document it gets.

11
00:00:37.094 --> 00:00:42.610
If the word count is zero or less, or over ten million, it throws, with a helpful message.

12
00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:44.693
Otherwise it hands the count back.

13
00:00:44.867 --> 00:00:45.934
It looks tiny.

14
00:00:45.934 --> 00:00:50.430
But C2, the JVM's optimizing compiler, doesn't count lines.

15
00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:55.764
It counts bytecode, the instructions javac writes into the class file.

16
00:00:55.764 --> 00:00:58.355
And this little check is 49 bytes.

17
00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:00.336
Now the kitchen.

18
00:01:00.336 --> 00:01:04.279
Copying a called method into its caller is called inlining.

19
00:01:04.279 --> 00:01:07.086
The sauce on page 12 is the called method.

20
00:01:07.086 --> 00:01:09.091
The main recipe is the caller.

21
00:01:09.091 --> 00:01:11.831
And chopping the onions for both at once?

22
00:01:11.831 --> 00:01:13.301
That's the real prize.

23
00:01:13.467 --> 00:01:15.559
Skipping the call is a small win.

24
00:01:15.559 --> 00:01:19.426
The big win is that C2's other tricks can now see both sides.

25
00:01:19.426 --> 00:01:22.723
It can work out values that are known ahead of time.

26
00:01:22.723 --> 00:01:28.428
It can prove that an object never leaves the method, so it never has to be created at all.

27
00:01:28.428 --> 00:01:30.204
It can reshape a whole loop.

28
00:01:30.204 --> 00:01:33.944
A method C2 doesn't copy in is a wall it can't see through.

29
00:01:34.867 --> 00:01:38.205
But a sensible cook only copies short sub-recipes.

30
00:01:38.205 --> 00:01:42.412
Nobody pastes a forty page wedding cake into the sandwich card.

31
00:01:42.412 --> 00:01:48.689
C2 keeps a budget too, and it decides at each call site, one call instruction in the bytecode.

32
00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:59.647
If the call runs on at least a quarter of the caller's runs, the site counts as hot, and C2 copies methods of up to 325 bytes.

33
00:01:59.647 --> 00:02:02.331
Otherwise, the limit is 35 bytes.

34
00:02:02.331 --> 00:02:06.316
These numbers are the same from JDK 21 to JDK 27.

35
00:02:06.467 --> 00:02:07.577
Now our check.

36
00:02:07.577 --> 00:02:08.291
49 bytes.

37
00:02:08.291 --> 00:02:13.762
A demo calls it from a method called summarize, on one call in eight.

38
00:02:13.762 --> 00:02:15.507
So the site isn't hot.

39
00:02:15.507 --> 00:02:17.014
Will C2 copy it in?

40
00:02:17.014 --> 00:02:18.283
Pause and guess.

41
00:02:21.333 --> 00:02:21.605
No.

42
00:02:21.605 --> 00:02:23.964
failed to inline: too big.

43
00:02:23.964 --> 00:02:28.137
49 is more than 35, so the check stays a wall.

44
00:02:29.067 --> 00:02:32.519
But where do 49 bytes come from, in a check this small?

45
00:02:32.519 --> 00:02:34.088
The test itself is short.

46
00:02:34.088 --> 00:02:42.248
Most of the bytes build the error message: two longs wrapped in Long objects, an array to carry them, and a call to String.format.

47
00:02:42.248 --> 00:02:47.269
Most of this check is a complaint letter, and the letter almost never gets sent.

48
00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.735
Here's where the kitchen picture breaks.

49
00:02:50.735 --> 00:02:55.741
A real cook would skip the page called, what to do if the kitchen catches fire.

50
00:02:55.741 --> 00:02:56.818
C2 skips nothing.

51
00:02:56.818 --> 00:03:00.493
It counts every byte, even the ones that almost never run.

52
00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:04.376
So let's give the cold path its own method.

53
00:03:04.376 --> 00:03:07.074
The check keeps the test and the throw.

54
00:03:07.074 --> 00:03:10.465
A new method, badWordCount, builds the exception.

55
00:03:10.465 --> 00:03:12.264
Now the check is 21 bytes.

56
00:03:12.264 --> 00:03:15.032
Same demo, same site, one call in eight.

57
00:03:15.032 --> 00:03:16.416
Will C2 copy it now?

58
00:03:19.067 --> 00:03:19.359
Yes.

59
00:03:19.359 --> 00:03:20.236
inline, hot.

60
00:03:20.236 --> 00:03:23.013
And don't read much into the word hot.

61
00:03:23.013 --> 00:03:28.055
It's just what C2 prints when it inlines and has nothing more to say.

62
00:03:28.055 --> 00:03:29.809
C2 is not a chatty cook.

63
00:03:30.733 --> 00:03:33.776
So that's how adding a method makes code faster.

64
00:03:33.776 --> 00:03:36.249
The new method is cold and rarely runs.

65
00:03:36.249 --> 00:03:42.017
The hot method got small enough to copy, and once it's copied, C2 can see right through it.

66
00:03:42.933 --> 00:03:44.961
Here it is in Java and in Kotlin.

67
00:03:44.961 --> 00:03:46.805
The hot path tests and throws.

68
00:03:46.805 --> 00:03:48.833
The cold path builds the message.

69
00:03:48.833 --> 00:03:56.699
We return the exception from it, so the throw stays in the hot method, where the next reader can see that the method ends there.

70
00:03:56.867 --> 00:03:58.206
One Kotlin twist.

71
00:03:58.206 --> 00:04:03.010
The Kotlin check with the message inside is 66 bytes, not 49.

72
00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:10.650
Kotlin's format is an inline function, so kotlinc pastes its body in before the JVM ever sees it.

73
00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:14.195
After the split, both languages are 21 bytes.

74
00:04:15.067 --> 00:04:16.140
A few guardrails.

75
00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:19.865
This matters in hot code, code that runs millions of times.

76
00:04:19.865 --> 00:04:22.644
Elsewhere, write the clearest check you can.

77
00:04:22.644 --> 00:04:27.316
Keep the message text the same, because someone's alert may search for it.

78
00:04:27.316 --> 00:04:31.420
And don't guess: the PrintInlining log tells you what C2 decided.

79
00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:34.413
C2 counts the bytes of the error path too.

80
00:04:34.413 --> 00:04:36.691
Give the cold path its own method.

81
00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:44.108
The moral of the story: C2 copies short sub-recipes into the main one, so it can chop for both at once.

82
00:04:44.108 --> 00:04:48.594
Short means bytes, not lines, and the bytes you rarely run still count.

83
00:04:48.594 --> 00:04:52.575
Keep the hot path short, and send the complaints down the hall.

84
00:04:52.733 --> 00:04:53.592
Quick check.

85
00:04:53.592 --> 00:04:56.741
A 30 byte method, called on one call in ten.

86
00:04:56.741 --> 00:05:00.748
And a 60 byte method, called on every run of its caller.

87
00:05:00.748 --> 00:05:02.680
Which ones does C2 copy in?

88
00:05:05.933 --> 00:05:06.339
Both.

89
00:05:06.339 --> 00:05:07.556
30 is under 35.

90
00:05:07.556 --> 00:05:12.668
And a call on every run is a hot site, where the budget is 325.

91
00:05:12.668 --> 00:05:20.296
Next: when the method behind a call depends on the object, how does C2 know which one to copy?
